Marine shell limestone in soybean development…

Introduction
In Brazil, the soybean (Glycine max L. Merrill) is extremely important to the
economy due to its production volume, making it the world's largest soybean producer.
It produces a significant 147.35 million tons of grain, corresponding to approximately
37% of global production. Considering soybeans and their byproducts, 126,677
thousand tons are exported (Empresa Brasileira de Pesquisa Agropecuária
[EMBRAPA], 2023). In the 2022/23 harvest, production grew 23.2%, with Mato
Grosso do Sul and Paraná seeing significant growth (Companhia Nacional de
Abastecimento [CONAB], 2023). The impact of this commodity on the production
chain is fundamental, reflecting on Brazilian cooperatives.
In 2023, the country had 4,509 cooperatives, 1,179 of which focused on
agribusiness. The number of cooperative members reached 23.45 million, representing
11.55% of the Brazilian population, a 14.5% increase compared to the previous year.
Furthermore, the cooperative sector generated 550,611 direct jobs, with a focus on the
agricultural sector, which employed 257,137 professionals (Organização das
Cooperativas Brasileiras [OCB], 2024).
The importance of soy in the food and nutritional field worldwide is reflected in
its high protein content (approximately 37%). With population growth, protein
consumption has increased at the same rate and proportion, corresponding to a need
for 20 million tons of protein per year, noting that much of this protein is of animal
origin. To meet this demand, it is necessary to find alternative protein sources, such as
soy-based foods. Chemically similar to animal protein, soy can have 14 times more
protein than chicken, four times more than eggs, and 3.5 times more than beef
(Norboeva & Xamrokulova, 2022). In addition to population growth, the vegan
population has gained notoriety, resulting in greater consumption of plant-based
protein (Qin, Wang, & Luo, 2022).
To meet high demand, grain production relies on large areas and soil fertilization.
To maximize production, fertilization is crucial because it meets the plant's nutritional
needs, primarily for macronutrients such as nitrogen (N), phosphorus (P), and
potassium (K). However, soils with low hydrogen potential (pH), i.e., more acidic
soils, compromise fertility due to the high levels of exchangeable aluminum (Al3+) and
hydrogen (H+) in the soil, which are toxic to most plants. Al3+ and H+ ions are largely
adsorbed to the soil colloid, preventing other elements important to the crop from
adsorbing to the soil colloid, resulting in a reduced cation exchange capacity (CEC).
To correct the pH of acidic soils, liming is performed to neutralize exchangeable
3+
Al in the topsoil and provide calcium (Ca) and magnesium (Mg). The application of
this mineral fertilizer improves nutrient availability for plants, enabling increased
yield and better development. The main types of limestone used in the field are
calcitic, dolomitic, or magnesian, classified according to the Ca and Mg content in
their composition ((EMBRAPA, 2021).
In the search for renewable and natural sources of limestone, seashells have
become an interesting alternative. Their composition is essentially calcium carbonate
(CaCO3), classified as calcitic limestone, and with an average relative neutralizing
power (RTP) of 71.44%. This raw material (seashells) is found in large quantities
along the coasts of southern Brazil.
The use of shell limestone in agricultural soils offers several benefits, including
correcting soil acidity, which creates a favorable environment for plant growth. Its
application improves soil structure, with regard to the number of macropores and
micropores, consequently increasing water and nutrient retention. Another benefit is
improved fertilizer efficiency and increased nutrient availability for plants, in addition
to having an immediate effect on the soil. Finally, the sustainable use of shell
limestone contributes to the recycling of marine waste, enabling more environmentally
friendly agricultural activities.
